FAIL SUMMARY - one defect, step 7 (secret hygiene), CD-j class (a swallowed OSError = fail-open)
F1 A secret Codex writes to a file whose HOST path exceeds PATH_MAX survives the scrub; the harness certifies
   the audit dir clean, writes VERDICT: PASS and exits 0.
   Repro (real harness from TREE, real container, probe stub parked; the planting runs INSIDE the checker as
   uid 1000 and reads the token from the read-only /codex-home/auth.json exactly as Codex could):
     flock $LOCK bash $D/hygdrive.sh $D $T hyg-deep-tree go deep
     plant: 25 nested dirs of 200 x 'd' under /out, then deep.txt = "deep <access_token>"
     -> "# harness rc=0", outputs "T.1.checker-codex.verdict" = "VERDICT: PASS", stderr has NO "deleted" line
     -> GNU `grep -rlF <token>` on the audit dir: swarm/codex/T.1/out/dddd.../deep.txt
     -> fd-based audit (os.fwalk/openat): "SECRET HITS: 1  CONTENT /out/ddd.../deep.txt (pathlen 5225)"
   Also reproduced with my copy's harness in the all-shapes run (hyg-all: rc=0, PASS, pathlen 5219).
   Docker-free: `tools.py secrets clean --auth fake $D/s7deep` -> "ok 0 0" (0 deleted, 0 remaining) while
   GNU grep -r still finds the token.
   Root cause (tools.py): sweep() `try: st = os.lstat(path) except OSError: continue` (lines ~537-540) - the
   child's lstat fails with errno 36 ENAMETOOLONG at host path length 4189, so the entry is silently skipped:
   not deleted, not counted in `remaining`, not logged. fix_perms() swallows the same error. remove_path()
   returns True ("really gone") when lstat fails, and os.path.lexists() returns False on ENAMETOOLONG, so even
   a drop() there would be reported as deleted.
   Brief step 7: "a file that still contains a secret or cannot be read/rewritten is DELETED (and its path
   logged to stderr) ... No secret may remain anywhere under the audit dir". This file cannot be read by the
   scrubber, is not deleted and is not logged, and the secret remains. Reachable in real use: under a real
   $AUD (~110 chars) any /out path deeper than ~4 kB, trivial for a prompt-injected Codex with the sandbox
   bypassed. The oracle does not plant this shape, and neither do the worker's tests.
   Remedy verified to work: the harness's EXISTING fail-closed fallback (`chmod -R u+rwX` + `rm -rf $AUD/out`)
   removes this tree - on $D/s7deep: rm rc=0, GNU grep -r afterwards finds 0. It never fires because the
   error is swallowed. So: count any lstat/listdir/open OSError except ENOENT as `remaining` (or walk with
   dir fds), and make remove_path report failure on a non-ENOENT lstat error. All in CD1 territory.

