OBSERVATIONS (not grounds for FAIL)
 O1 A user xattr (os.setxattr from inside the container, user.tok = the access token) on an out/ file
    survives redaction. The brief names content forms only (raw, escaped, decoded), and git does not archive
    xattrs; `cp -a`/tar would carry it. Cheap fix: clear xattrs during the scrub.
 O2 A base64-encoded token in out/ is not recognised. Out of the brief's forms; noted for the backlog.
 O3 ORACLE GAPS (lead's artifact): the oracle alone cannot see M3 (snapshot), M4a (chmod) or M4b
    (delete-on-failure); only the worker's suite pins them. It plants no >PATH_MAX path (F1). Suggested
    additions: an in-place auth rotation during the run, a deep /out path, and a file the scrubber cannot
    rewrite.
 O4 SIGHUP DETAIL reads "harness failed (exit 0) before producing a result" while the process exits 129
    (cosmetic; the skip, teardown and scrub all happen).
 O5 Refresh-token observation (lead asked for a view). A read-only auth.json keeps the host file
    byte-identical, but it cannot stop the real Codex inside from spending the refresh token at
    auth.openai.com. If the provider rotates refresh tokens (one-time use, reuse detection), the new pair lives
    only in the container's memory and is lost. The host keeps a spent refresh token, and the user's own host
    login can go stale or be revoked - worse than the contract's "a later auth skip". This is an availability
    risk, not a disclosure risk. I could not verify it without the real Codex. A mitigation within the
    contract: before starting the container, read the snapshot's refresh timestamp and skip `auth` when a
    refresh is due, rather than letting the container refresh.
 O6 Docker: counts back to baseline (12 containers / 10 networks / 8 volumes). I removed 10 dangling images
    whose Config.Cmd is tinyproxy, by ID (the oracle's re-tag trap plus my bogus-conf build). The fixed-name
    images remain.
